{"event_type":"review","id":"4ba090bc7676d953939b78405ed28e4ae928d9184144ba7c4389cd5bcb14a359","occurred_at":"2026-11-12T09:40:04Z","payload":{"artifacts":[{"location":"/home/ctaylor/.local/share/crq-backfill/artifacts/sha256/f7b35c14d633b6ab92abbb5ca8a3b50f0724970885137e550d62f22efeb3a2e8","sha256":"f7b35c14d633b6ab92abbb5ca8a3b50f0724970885137e550d62f22efeb3a2e8"},{"location":"/home/ctaylor/.local/share/crq-backfill/artifacts/sha256/e8fee83bdf62586b941b68aa90a835bb9380f3db6081a6640ab3ba74897ef8d2","sha256":"e8fee83bdf62586b941b68aa90a835bb9380f3db6081a6640ab3ba74897ef8d2"},{"kind":"git-bundle","location":"/home/ctaylor/.local/share/crq-backfill/artifacts/sha256/7deb07b4636b1513611519b01eaafbaa1a7d6a9b45bd131bc1813025670b3128","sha256":"7deb07b4636b1513611519b01eaafbaa1a7d6a9b45bd131bc1813025670b3128"},{"location":"/home/ctaylor/.local/share/crq-backfill/artifacts/sha256/99cc55f80568c4cd848d0309c3f22d886866c591e5273aa7769ecbda1708df70","sha256":"99cc55f80568c4cd848d0309c3f22d886866c591e5273aa7769ecbda1708df70"},{"location":"/home/ctaylor/.local/share/crq-backfill/artifacts/sha256/54097f7959ee342d3016e554a092339d8fe0530cc6d1c7e64fc610c7d80cdec9","sha256":"54097f7959ee342d3016e554a092339d8fe0530cc6d1c7e64fc610c7d80cdec9"}],"attempt_id":"00542ccbf88ccf04d0583a818e3dd67a6219ca35c34a7c880bc38800641be198:0:1","base":"8da5cf469d93096944945a66bc447d4b4aba4cf5","completion":{"errors":[],"exit_code":0,"scope_verified":true,"skipped":false,"terminal":true},"findings":[{"anchor_status":"verified","blob":"6f12047852b830ed4da5c95b19c11adb33865593","body":"**Give `input_scope` and `reviewed_scope` separate lists in `_review`.**\n\n`requested_scope`, `input_scope`, and `reviewed_scope` all reference the same `rows` list (lines 115–117). The `reviewed-exceeds-requested` and `reviewed-exceeds-input` cases edit a scope in place (lines 465–468), so the edit changes all three scopes. After the edit, the reviewed scope equals the requested and input scopes, so the named containment violation is absent. A replay may then pass without enforcing those containment rules.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🐛 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n         \"requested_scope\": rows,\n-        \"input_scope\": rows,\n-        \"reviewed_scope\": rows,\n+        \"input_scope\": copy.deepcopy(rows),\n+        \"reviewed_scope\": copy.deepcopy(rows),\n```\n\n</details>","id":"517b916714b1a1f1af60b84ddee5a5a201f9db34fb59d1d1888299f15570597d","intervals":[[115,117]],"path":"apps/crq-python/ledger_vector_emit_b1.py","severity":"minor","source_id":null,"status":"untriaged","title":"Give `inputscope` and `reviewedscope` separate lists in `review`."},{"anchor_status":"verified","blob":"be6b00bfb929170a8498f87b2e5606c0a3641f77","body":"**Snapshot the run record before the second preparation call.**\n\n`record` is an alias of the queue entry, not a copy. If the second `prepare_current_job` call changes that dict in place, `record` changes with it. `record_after` is a deepcopy taken after the second call, so `idempotent_record_unchanged` (`record_after == record`) stays true after an in-place change. Copy the record before the second call so the comparison has an independent snapshot.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed change</summary>\n\n```diff\n-            record = queue[\"items\"][0][\"repair_runs\"][run]\n+            record = copy.deepcopy(queue[\"items\"][0][\"repair_runs\"][run])\n```\n\n</details>","id":"9d6a24fd595574e9632f7458ff8d9a10c7c1da7790351aedcc84f548197e8ec2","intervals":[[229,229]],"path":"apps/crq-python/repair_prepare_vectors.py","severity":"minor","source_id":null,"status":"untriaged","title":"Snapshot the run record before the second preparation call."}],"head":"153b9dc4be9bb6b9721e4a122257ade45a63a037","head_tree":"e808aac5adf61e3f0f06365372f81340b3333575","input_scope":[{"blob":"599bdb7b16b3dc4e7a204b7b54832fea4247cfb6","intervals":[[1,1074]],"path":"apps/crq-python/finding_inbox_vector_emit_b1.py"},{"blob":"6f12047852b830ed4da5c95b19c11adb33865593","inter